{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-9920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-9920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-9920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d84328fab436a040bdd922cd9ec8eec5495237611f406340103852ec75f60a1d"} {"question" "q-9920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-9920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in [-1,1)"] ["math" "x\\in [0,1)"] ["math" "x\\in [0,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-9920" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{e(n+1)} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d84328fab436a040bdd922cd9ec8eec5495237611f406340103852ec75f60a1d"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-1,1)"] ["math" "x\\in (-1,\\infty )"] ["math" "x\\in (-2,2)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)"] ["math" "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-12409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}=\\frac{x}{2\\cdot 2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}\\cdot 5}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}\\cdot 8}+⋯\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+1}(3n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}(3n-1)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(3n-1)}{2(3n+2)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3n-1}{3n+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{2}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<2.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $2,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-2,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=2,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-2$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{8}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparision test, whith ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{3n-1}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3n-1})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{3n-1}=\\frac{1}{3},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3n-1}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So the endpoint $x=2$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-2)^{n}}{{2}^{n}(3n-1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{8}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-2$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d84328fab436a040bdd922cd9ec8eec5495237611f406340103852ec75f60a1d"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\in (-2,2)"] ["math" "x\\in (-4,4]"] ["math" "x\\in [-2,2)"] ["math" "x\\in [-2,2]"] ["math" "x\\in [-4,4)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-125292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\in [-2,2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the interval of convergence for the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}=\\frac{x}{4\\cdot 3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}\\cdot 8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{4}^{3}\\cdot 13}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{4}^{n+1}(5n+3)}\\cdot \\frac{{4}^{n}(5n-2)}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x(5n-2)}{4(5n+3)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n-2}{5n+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{|x|}{4}<1\\,⟹\\,|x|<4.\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $4,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-4,4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=4,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-4$ and $x=4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{13}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see if this series converges, we use the limit comparison test, with ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ We observe that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are positive, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5n-2})}{(\\frac{1}{n})}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{5n-2}=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is positive and finite. Hence, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ either *both* converge or *both* diverge.\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is divergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{5n-2}$ is also divergent by the limit comparison test. So, the endpoint $x=4$ is not included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4)^{n}}{{4}^{n}(5n-2)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{5n-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{13}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So, the endpoint $x=-4$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-4,4),$ or equivalently, $-4\\le x<4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d84328fab436a040bdd922cd9ec8eec5495237611f406340103852ec75f60a1d"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-347514" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2x}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+4}{n+5})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d84328fab436a040bdd922cd9ec8eec5495237611f406340103852ec75f60a1d"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061653/state.json" "file_sha256" "7c52a3029ad509af19688a06ade7ed9b781997797635122331ee22508d0ca67c"} {"question" "q-353173" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value"